## Building a Module Hierarchy

### The Tree Structure

```xiom
mod game {
  pub mod player {
    pub type Character {
      name: Str;
      health: Int;
    }

    pub fn create(name: Str) -> Character {
      Character{ name: name, health: 100 }
    }

    pub fn heal(c: &mut Character, amount: Int) {
      c.health += amount;
      if c.health > 100 { c.health = 100; };
    }
  }

  pub mod world {
    pub type Location {
      name: Str;
      danger_level: Int;
    }

    pub fn describe(loc: &Location) -> Str {
      loc.name.clone()
    }
  }

  pub mod combat {
    pub fn attack_damage(attacker_level: Int, weapon_power: Int) -> Int {
      attacker_level * weapon_power
    }
  }
}
```

### Accessing Deep Modules

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  use game;
  let mut hero = game.player.create("Aria");
  game.player.heal(&mut hero, 20);
  io.println(hero.health);
  return 0;
}
```

### Module Re-exports for Clean API

```xiom
mod game {
  pub mod player { /* ... */ }
  pub mod world { /* ... */ }

  pub use player::create as create_player;
  pub use world::Location;
}

// Now: game.create_player("Aria") — no submodule path needed!
```

